```
ROLE: theory_skeptic  VOTE: yellow
MUNDANE_EXPLANATIONS:
  - Sorting two DIFFERENT observed sets gives different positional indices for shared elements — trivial set-theoretic consequence, fully documented as astype('category')'s sort-over-observed-unique-values contract. No emergence; the f=0 null boundary (full alphabet -> 0/50 differ) is the textbook prediction.
  - deltaR2 rising monotonically in overlap fraction f is mechanically expected: more shared categories with mismatched codes -> more corruption of a feature the tree model is told to split on. A permutation-test baseline (replace shared codes with uniform random ints of matched cardinality) would predict the same R2 collapse to ~negative.
  - R2 going NEGATIVE for tree regressors fed misaligned integer codes is a basic property of axis-aligned splitters on label-scrambled features; not a discovery about pandas, a discovery about trees.
  - The pyarrow "first-seen order" arm is the same phenomenon under a different sort key (arrival order vs lex); calling it a "mechanistically distinct 2nd arm" overstates — both are "sort key depends on shard contents."
CONFOUNDS:
  - The framing "silent ML feature contract failure" conflates the mechanical fact (codes mismatch) with the sociotechnical claim (a real consumer reads .cat.codes cross-shard as a stable feature). The L0 demonstrates the former; the latter is asserted, not measured. R2 collapse is conditional on a consumer that ACTUALLY uses raw .cat.codes as input — an assumption, not a tested condition.
  - HGB and RF are both tree-based; "not model-specific" within axis-aligned splitter family is weak. A linear model on one-hot would not exhibit this; the claim's generality across model classes is overstated by the cross-check.
ASSUMPTIONS_UNTESTED:
  - That any production pipeline actually consumes raw .cat.codes from storage-cast shards as a model feature (vs. re-encoding with a fitted OrdinalEncoder/category_encoders on read). This is the load-bearing sociotechnical assumption and it is asserted on prior-art grounds, not measured.
  - That defensive practices (pd.Categorical(categories=fixed_list), pa.dictionary with shared dict, downstream fit-on-train encoder) do NOT catch this in real pipelines. No prevalence evidence.
  - That f (overlap fraction) in real partitioned datasets falls in the 0.1-0.4 "smoking gun" band rather than ~0 (fully disjoint, codes overlap by accident — different bug) or ~1 (full alphabet, null exit). The realistic-f distribution for time/geo shards is unmeasured.
STATISTICAL_GAPS:
  - 12 seeds is adequate for mean deltaR2 but the f=0.6 "high-variance tail" is disclosed without a CI or seed-distribution plot; strict monotonicity holds only 0->0.4 by the packet's own wording, so the "monotonic smoking gun" is really "monotonic on [0, 0.4], non-monotonic beyond."
  - Single synthetic generator (50 cats, theta_k~N(0,1), y=theta+N(0,0.5)). No sensitivity to category count, latent-effect skewness, or y-noise. R2~0.78 is "realistic" for THIS dgp; deltaR2 magnitude is dgp-specific.
  - No baseline measuring "what deltaR2 would you get if you simply permuted the codes of shared categories uniformly at random" — this would isolate whether the observed deltaR2 exceeds the trivial-scramble prediction or merely matches it. Without that, the effect size is uninterpretable as evidence of anything beyond "shared codes are scrambled."
RATIONALE: The empirical execution is clean (composition verified on pandas 3.0.3, exact f=0 null boundary, parquet round-trip, RF cross-check, could-fail audits), and the orchestrator's anti-rigging discipline is real. But from a strict theory lens nothing in the L0 is surprising: astype('category') sorting per-shard is documented behavior (per the packet itself), the cross-shard mismatch follows by definition of "sort," and tree-regressor R2 collapse on misaligned integer codes is predicted by elementary splitter theory. The packet's own framing concedes the per-file sort is documented; the novelty claim rests entirely on the sociotechnical "no doc warns it's hazardous CROSS-SHARD as an ML feature" leg, which is a coverage gap in documentation, not a theoretical surprise. The pyarrow arm is the same mechanism under a different sort key, not a mechanistically distinct second source. Load-bearing assumption — that a real consumer reads raw .cat.codes cross-shard — is untested at L0 (acknowledged: L1 would test it). YELLOW, not RED: the phenomenon is real and the experiment is honest; the gating question is altitude (theory-predicted vs. non-obvious-coupling), which is stress-test B's domain, not mine. Recommend L1 must include (i) a permutation-baseline showing deltaR2 exceeds trivial-scramble prediction, (ii) a non-tree model class to bound generality, (iii) evidence of a real consumer pattern that reads .cat.codes cross-shard.
```
